<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tōtōmi Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tōtōmi Province was an old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the western part of present-day Shizuoka Prefecture, around Lake Hamana. Long ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-clan">Imagawa clan</a>, it was contested after 1560 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, who took most of it and moved his base to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a> in 1570. Shingen defeated Ieyasu there at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mikatagahara">Battle of Mikatagahara</a> in 1573.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/totomi-province.jpg" alt="Tōtōmi Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Totomi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tōtōmi Province was a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the area of what is today western Shizuoka Prefecture. It bordered on Mikawa, Suruga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinano-province">Shinano Provinces</a>, and its abbreviated name was Enshū. Its name derives from the old name of Lake Hamana. It was one of the original provinces established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> period under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiho-code">Taihō Code</a>, and its first capital stood in what is now Iwata, at a place called Mitsuke, a name that survived as Mitsuke-juku, a post station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tōkaidō</a>. Under the Engishiki it was ranked a &quot;superior country&quot; in importance and a &quot;middle country&quot; in distance from the capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early Muromachi period the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-clan">Imagawa clan</a> ruled it nominally before it passed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiba-clan">Shiba clan</a>, but in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> the Imagawa recovered it and effectively annexed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suruga-province">Suruga Province</a>. After the Imagawa were defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-okehazama">Battle of Okehazama</a>, Tōtōmi was divided between the warlords <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> of Kai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> of Mikawa. Ieyasu built Hamamatsu Castle, which became the capital of the province in practice, and parts of Tōtōmi stayed contested between Tokugawa and Takeda until Shingen's death.</p><p>After the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> was forced to exchange his domains in the Tōkai region for the Kantō. Hamamatsu went to the Horii clan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakegawa-castle">Kakegawa Castle</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamauchi-kazutoyo">Yamauchi Kazutoyo</a>. Once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> was established the Tokugawa recovered their lost lands and assigned Tōtōmi to various fudai daimyōs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tōkaidō</a> road from Edo to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> passed through the province, and for defence the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> forbade bridges over the major rivers such as the Tenryū, which led to towns growing up at the river crossings. At the end of the shogunate the province was divided among several domains.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> the last shōgun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</a>, returned to Suruga in 1868 to rule the short-lived Shizuoka Domain, and the daimyōs of Tōtōmi were reassigned. After the han system was abolished in 1871 the province's prefectures were merged into Hamamatsu Prefecture, which became part of Shizuoka Prefecture on 21 August 1876. Many unemployed former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> settled in the Makinohara region and developed the green <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> industry. With the Tōkaidō Main Line railway Hamamatsu grew into a major commercial and industrial centre, especially through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>-spinning industries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tōtōmi Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ujichika expanded into <b>Totomi</b> and issued one of the earliest Sengoku house codes, the Imagawa kana mokuroku of 1526.</blockquote><a href="/state/imagawa-clan-domain">Imagawa clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Shingen conquered Shinano and parts of Suruga, Kozuke and <b>Totomi</b>, fighting Uesugi Kenshin five times at Kawanakajima.</blockquote><a href="/state/takeda-clan-domain">Takeda clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Ieyasu unified Mikawa, crushing an Ikko uprising, and took <b>Totomi</b>, moving to Hamamatsu in 1570.</blockquote><a href="/state/tokugawa-clan-domain">Tokugawa clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
